<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cuban Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cuban Revolution was the armed rising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> and his 26th of July Movement against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulgencio-batista">Fulgencio Batista</a>. It began with the attack on the Moncada Barracks in 1953, and from 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> fought from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-maestra">Sierra Maestra</a>. Batista fled on 1 January 1959, and Castro's government soon allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> into a communist state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution.jpg" alt="Cuban Revolution" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fidel_Castro_and_his_men_in_the_Sierra_Maestra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…logistical support, suspended the constitution; his Bureau for the Repression of Communist Activities carried out wide-scale violence, torture and public executions, the murders mounting in 1957, with estimates of the dead ranging from hundreds to about 20,000, until the <b>Cuban Revolution</b> forced him into exile in December 1958.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Exiled after 1955, Perón moved further left, praised the <b>Cuban Revolution</b> and liberation theology, and in 1956 gave John William Cooke command of the movement in Argentina; Cooke presented it as “antibureaucratic, socialist, profoundly national” and in Cuba combined it with Guevarism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/peronism">Peronism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words answer the farewell letter that Che Guevara, a commander of the <b>Cuban Revolution</b>, left when he departed Cuba to spread revolution in the Congo and later in Bolivia, where he was captured and killed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hasta-siempre-comandante">Hasta Siempre, Comandante</a></li></ul></section>
